%9Setting:%9

    update_check (boolean; default: OFF)

%9Description:%9

    Checks once a day whether a newer signed release of erssi Shellter
    Edition is there, and says so - once for each new release - in the
    window of client messages (the Notices window of erssi, the status
    window of irssi):

        erssi 2026.11.1 is available (signed). Install it: /update
        install (or erssi --update in a shell), then /upgrade
          A short summary of what the release brings.

    It is off by default because each check contacts the erssi
    repository (github.com, unless erssi was installed from another
    repository: the REPO of its shellter-install.conf). The
    first check runs a minute after erssi starts, then one every 24 hours.
    The time of the last check is kept in ~/.erssi/update-check.state
    (mode 0600), so a restart or /UPGRADE does not check again before the
    day is over; two erssi with the same home share it.

    The summary (up to three lines) comes from the signed tag of the
    release; it is kept in the state file and shown again by /UPDATE.

    The check is the one /UPDATE CHECK makes (/HELP update). Nothing is
    installed: only /UPDATE INSTALL does, when you give it. A check that fails (no network, GitHub down) is not shown;
    after three failures in a row one line says so, and /UPDATE shows
    why the last one failed. A release tag that
    moved to another commit is shown at once: report it.

    The shipped config marks no activity for client messages, so from
    another window the line is easy to miss. The statusbar item "update"
    shows the new version while there is one (and nothing otherwise). It
    is not in the shipped statusbars; to add it to the info bar:

        /STATUSBAR ADDITEM -after lag update info

    With no installer installed with erssi (a distribution package, a
    build of your own), for an install of another user (a system install
    made by root) and in Capsicum capability mode the setting does
    nothing, and says why when it is turned on.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET update_check ON
    /UPDATE

%9See also:%9 UPDATE, UPGRADE, STATUSBAR

